\ `to` on a name that is neither a declared local nor an existing word: at the
\ REPL it auto-creates a global, inside a definition it declares a word-local.

\ auto-create on first assignment, then read
4 to x
x . cr                                  \ 4

\ reassignment of the auto-created global
9 to x
x . cr                                  \ 9

\ holds any value, not just floats
[ 1 2 3 ] to arr
arr . cr                                \ [ 1 2 3 ]

\ explicit `variable` still works alongside auto-create
variable v
5 to v
v . cr                                  \ 5

\ === inside a definition the name becomes a local ===

\ no locals list at all: the first `to` opens the frame where it stands
: implicit 1 to fresh  fresh 41 + ;
implicit . cr                           \ 42

\ captures are just `to` on the incoming values, topmost first
: hypotenuse to b to a  a a * b b * + sqrt ;
3 4 hypotenuse . cr                     \ 5

\ the slot is per call, not shared like a global would be
: counted 0 to tally  tally 1 + to tally  tally ;
counted . cr                            \ 1
counted . cr                            \ 1

\ intermediates chain, and a head list that receives grows to hold them
: chained | n | n 2 * to doubled  doubled 1 + to bumped  doubled bumped + ;
5 chained . cr                          \ 21

\ a loop body assigns the same slot each turn
: summed | n | 0 to total  0 to i
	begin i n < while  total i + to total  i 1+ to i  repeat
	total ;
5 summed . cr                           \ 10

\ a global of the same name minted later leaves the compiled local alone
7 to tally
counted . cr                            \ 1
tally . cr                              \ 7

\ code may precede the frame: the literal is emitted, then the frame, then the store
: after-code [: 3 :] execute to sprout  sprout 1 + ;
after-code . cr                         \ 4

\ a quotation declares into its own frame, not the enclosing word's
: quotation-frame [: 5 to inner  inner inner + :] execute ;
quotation-frame . cr                    \ 10

clear
\ === errors ===
\ a read of an undeclared name is still unknown — only `to` declares
: reads-unknown never-set 1 + ;

